%description:

RFC 9293 — the checksum is never optional: the sender generates it, and the value is the one
the document defines.

Checks RFC9293-CKSUM-1 (must, MUST-2) from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9293/catalog.md, the half that level 2 could not reach: not
that a field is present, but that it holds the value RFC 9293 defines. The English procedure
is in doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks/checksum.md#checksum-by-default; the program
steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the plain mockup: host A = host1,
host B = host2.

Host A is the subject: nothing in the scenario says anything about its checksum, so it sends
in the state a host has when no one configures it. Host B is a control: it is told to compute
the value the document defines. The two let the program tell two failures apart. If step 1
fails, the arithmetic in this test is wrong. If step 1 passes and step 2 fails, the
arithmetic is right and the default state of a host is the finding.

The value each step compares against is computed from the segment on the wire, over a pseudo
header of the two addresses, the TCP header with a zero checksum field, and the data; see
TcpMutations.h, expectedTcpChecksum. It is the same computation the model itself performs in
TcpChecksumInsertionHook::computeChecksum.

Expected result: FAIL. The `checksumMode` parameter of the ~Tcp module
(src/inet/transportlayer/tcp/Tcp.ned:189) is `default("declared")`. In that mode no checksum
is computed at all: the field stays zero and the header carries a flag that asserts it
correct, which `Tcp::checkChecksum` then believes without arithmetic. RFC 9293 says the
checksum is never optional. Step 2 therefore fails while step 1 passes. See
doc/project/evidence/model/tcp/results.md.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "TcpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// True when the checksum field of the segment holds the value RFC 9293 defines for it.
static bool carriesTheRfc9293Checksum(const Packet *frame)
{
    return tcpChecksumField(frame) == expectedTcpChecksum(frame);
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc9293_checksum_default")
        // Step 1, the control: host B was told to compute the checksum, and the value on the
        // wire is the one the document defines. This step establishes that the arithmetic
        // below is right, so that step 2 speaks about the model and not about the test.
        .once(on("host2.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.srcPort == 1000")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return carriesTheRfc9293Checksum(c.event.packet); })
                  .describe("host B's segment, whose checksum is the value RFC 9293 defines")
                  .within(1.0))
        // Observation 1: host A's segment carries that same value, with nothing configured.
        .once(on("host1.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.destPort == 1000")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return carriesTheRfc9293Checksum(c.event.packet); })
                  .describe("host A's checksum is the value RFC 9293 defines, with nothing configured")
                  .within(1.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9293_checksum_default compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The plain mockup: host A -- link L -- host B.
network Rfc9293DefaultNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc9293DefaultNet
sim-time-limit = 2s
seed-set = 0

# Static ARP keeps the wire free of ARP request/reply noise.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# Nothing is said about host A's checksum: that absence is the subject of the check.
# Host B is the control, and it is told to compute the value the document defines.
*.host2.tcp.checksumMode = "computed"

# The scenario constants of checks.md#common-mockups. Host B only receives: echoFactor = 0
# keeps the reverse direction free of data, so that the segments from host B are pure
# acknowledgments and an observation about them cannot be confused with an echo.
*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 5000B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = 0.4s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpEchoApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000
*.host2.app[0].echoFactor = 0

%# expected-result: FAIL
%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9293_checksum_default: PASS
